00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.420
So we're here with Mike Kavis.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:39.920
Hi, Mike.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:40.670
How are you doing?

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:41.140
MIKE KAVIS: Very good.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:42.630
It's been a great show so far.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:43.588
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:04:43.588 --> 00:04:45.517
And you work for Cloud
technology partners?

00:04:45.517 --> 00:04:47.100
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah,
we're a Boston based

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.235
firm that helps companies
get to the cloud,

00:04:49.235 --> 00:04:52.710
whether they're migrating
apps, or building net new.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.250
And we do a lot of work
on the Google Platform.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:55.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.168
Could you tell us
a little bit more

00:04:57.168 --> 00:04:59.260
about what kind of
products you use with them?

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.230
And what is your
favorite product,

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:04.890
or the favorite product for
your customers, actually?

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.473
MIKE KAVIS: Well,
my personal favorite

00:05:06.473 --> 00:05:09.020
is the whole big
data suite of things,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.970
you know, Dataflow,
Pub/Sub, BigQuery.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.060
I mean, most-- You
know, I've been

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.390
working in data
warehousing my whole life.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:19.280
And the hardest part is
always getting the data in.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.040
And at Google, it's just
a couple APIs and a couple

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.290
configurations, and
the hard part's done.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.930
And then you actually focus
on getting the results out

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:28.855
of the data.

00:05:28.855 --> 00:05:30.850
That's a game
changer in my eyes.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:32.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:36.260
So I assuming, you also work
with Bigtable a little bit.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:37.635
What do you think
about Bigtable?

00:05:37.635 --> 00:05:39.420
It's quite a new product.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:40.719
Have you used it?

00:05:40.719 --> 00:05:42.635
MIKE KAVIS: Well, I mean,
again, my background

00:05:42.635 --> 00:05:43.790
is data warehousing.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:46.140
I remember buying
appliances like Netezza

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:47.590
that costs zillions of dollars.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.220
And you go dark for a
year, just setting up

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.510
the infrastructure and stuff.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.870
And now you get tools
like BigQuery, Bigtable,

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.660
and you're just up and running.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.330
And getting results that
are 10 times faster than you

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.430
can get any place else.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.485
It's just-- It's just
kind of amazing, actually.

00:06:02.485 --> 00:06:03.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, wonderful.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.130
And so I believe you're
here at GCP Next.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:07.280
You presented?

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.490
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah, I
was on a panel talking

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.960
about cloud migrations,
which is kind

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.790
of a new chapter
for Google, right?

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.660
I mean, originally it
was all about the future

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:19.716
of development, with all
these high level services.

00:06:19.716 --> 00:06:21.090
But I think the
realization comes

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.089
is you've got to get
people on a platform first.

00:06:23.089 --> 00:06:25.250
So now there's a big
focus on cloud migration.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:26.986
We do a lot of
work in that space.

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:27.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Very interesting.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:29.250
MARK MANDEL: When you
say cloud migration,

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.130
is that specifically like
moving from one cloud

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:31.930
provider to another?

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:32.660
What does that really mean?

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:34.409
MIKE KAVIS: It could
be, but normally it's

00:06:34.409 --> 00:06:36.330
moving from on-prem
to the cloud.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:40.130
And the biggest use case is
always we have 20 data centers.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.610
We've got to get to
3 by x date, which

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.011
is usually very aggressive.

00:06:44.011 --> 00:06:44.760
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:46.500
MIKE KAVIS: So
the good is people

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:47.940
are moving fast to the cloud.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.864
The bad is they're
lifting and shifting,

00:06:49.864 --> 00:06:51.780
so they're not getting
advantage of the cloud.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.130
But that's the next wave.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.505
Once you get them
there, then you

00:06:54.505 --> 00:06:57.479
start helping them
re-architect or build net New.

00:06:57.479 --> 00:06:58.895
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Very interesting.

00:06:58.895 --> 00:07:01.320
So for people that are doing
that shifting and lifting,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.640
I'm assuming that most of them
did just move to Google Compute

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:05.472
Engine.

00:07:05.472 --> 00:07:06.180
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.650
So in a lot of cases again,
they're time crunched.

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:10.910
So it's get what I
have in the cloud.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:13.792
So they're treating Google more
like a virtual data center.

00:07:13.792 --> 00:07:14.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I see.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:17.600
MIKE KAVIS: And they don't have
the time yet, to take advantage

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.025
of those higher level services.

00:07:19.025 --> 00:07:20.400
That's-- Once you
get them there,

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.534
then you move them up the stack.

00:07:22.534 --> 00:07:24.700
MARK MANDEL: It's a foot
in the door type situation.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:27.262
FRANCESC CAMPOY: When you
say move them up the stack,

00:07:27.262 --> 00:07:28.970
could you tell everybody
more about that?

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.230
Is it like a container
engine, or do you

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.775
move them to App Engine?

00:07:32.775 --> 00:07:34.230
How does that work?

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:34.770
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah,
that's a loaded term.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.686
And it means so many
things to so many people.

00:07:36.686 --> 00:07:39.490
What I really mean is getting
them to use the more high value

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:43.120
API, so getting them
to use Kubernetes,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.160
getting them to use
BigQuery, Dataflow,

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.200
and all those services
where you no longer have

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.690
to focus on the infrastructure
and the plumbing.

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:52.380
It kind of does it for you.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:54.796
And then you can focus on
building apps and doing

00:07:54.796 --> 00:07:56.170
the machine
learning, and getting

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:57.694
insights, and stuff like that.

00:07:57.694 --> 00:07:58.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.407
That is actually a little bit
of what Urs was mentioning there

00:08:01.407 --> 00:08:03.490
in the keynote, about the
serverless architecture.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.990
Not because there's no service,
but because you don't really

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:07.150
care about them anymore.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:07.470
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:08.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
was very interesting.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.010
MARK MANDEL: Really moving up
to another level of abstraction.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:11.010
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah,
and the challenge is,

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.050
most of these enterprises
are just figuring out

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.750
what cloud is, so
you start talking

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.210
about serverless stuff, their
eyes just kind of glaze over.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:21.770
And sometimes it takes
them stumbling and fumbling

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:24.840
on the cloud for a couple years
till they get it and start

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.130
moving up the value chain
and taking those high level

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:28.630
services.

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.280
The companies that have been
in the cloud for a while,

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:30.860
they get it.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:35.243
And they're salivating
over new stuff like that.

00:08:35.243 --> 00:08:36.909
MARK MANDEL: So
speaking of the keynote,

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.179
did you have a particular
launch or product or demo?

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:41.090
What was your favorite part?

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:43.809
MIKE KAVIS: I liked a lot
of the machine learning

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:45.429
prediction stuff.

00:08:45.429 --> 00:08:46.220
I liked that a lot.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:48.506
We're getting a lot of projects.

00:08:48.506 --> 00:08:50.130
You know, sometimes
you're labeled IoT.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:51.730
Sometimes you're
labeled big data.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.600
But there's a lot
of companies who are

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.940
harvesting sensor information.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:58.410
And their first step is just
to get it all in one place.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.090
But their ultimate goal is, what
can we learn from this data?

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:02.950
And how can we
offer new services?

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.530
How can we change an industry?

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.750
Or how can we change
pricing models?

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.320
So a lot of companies are
early in that journey.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:14.262
And you know, we're helping
them get the data in one place.

00:09:14.262 --> 00:09:15.720
Because right now,
machine learning

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.210
is an art and a science.

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:17.960
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.727
MIKE KAVIS: And you're trying to
make that so any developer can

00:09:20.727 --> 00:09:21.070
kind of tap into that.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:21.820
MARK MANDEL: Sort
of a commodity.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:22.625
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah.

00:09:22.625 --> 00:09:24.120
And I'm looking forward to that.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.050
I know there's a lot
of work yet to do.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.502
But that's a good
direction to be going.

00:09:28.502 --> 00:09:29.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
