NIELS PROVOS: It took
a while to prepare.

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:07.010
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:08.020
Yeah, I bet it did.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:10.980
Do you want to give us a
really quick 30-second synopsis

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:12.549
of what you just
presented on stage?

00:10:12.549 --> 00:10:13.340
NIELS PROVOS: Sure.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.010
So in Google, I'm responsible
for security and privacy

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.100
engineering.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.970
Google has spent many,
many years creating

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.880
a very, very secure platform.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.510
And so for GCP customers
who are wondering,

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:27.390
what does that mean for us?

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:30.200
So during that talk I
essentially said trust

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.560
and transparency is
very important to us.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.070
Let me explain to
you how we have

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:37.650
built Google's infrastructure
to be secure, and then relay

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.140
to you what that means
as a customer for running

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.480
on top of GCP.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:44.760
And so I essentially started
from the bottom-- trusted

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:48.730
hardware, trusted boot,
cryptographic identities

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:53.910
for our jobs, DDoS protection,
unfishible user identities

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.230
with [INAUDIBLE] second
factor, and then gave

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.090
some examples of how
customers can leverage that

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:01.830
on top of GCP.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:03.730
For example, storage
encryption happens

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:05.910
by default. For
Google App Engine,

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.690
we give you our Cloud
Security Scanner,

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:11.491
to find vulnerabilities such as
Access S or mixed content, mix

00:11:11.491 --> 00:11:11.990
pinning.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:14.050
I talked a little
bit about our efforts

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:18.010
to secure the TLS
certificate infrastructure,

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:19.850
with certificate
transparency, where

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:23.580
we have worked for years to
create a model where all issued

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.840
certificates can be verified
in a publicly available lock.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.140
And sort of just ran people
through the security stamps

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.660
they get for running on
GCP, and some of the things

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.960
that we are thinking of
giving them in the future.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.510
So for example, we are working
on a key management system

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:39.635
and those kind of things.

00:11:39.635 --> 00:11:40.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:42.540
That sounds like a
lot of information.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:46.280
So if anyone is more interested,
the keynote was recorded.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.340
And you should definitely
check the video out.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.067
I was actually checking
it out while we were here.

00:11:52.067 --> 00:11:53.400
It was a very good presentation.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.110
Congratulations on that.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:57.780
There's a specific topic that
we get a question quite often,

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:59.207
which is DDoS.

00:11:59.207 --> 00:12:00.040
NIELS PROVOS: Right.

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:01.873
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Can
you tell me a bit more?

00:12:01.873 --> 00:12:04.260
Where is that DDoS
protection coming and taking

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.500
place for Google Cloud Platform?

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:09.240
NIELS PROVOS: So when
you run on our platform,

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.690
you essentially benefit from
our serving infrastructure

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:13.020
as a network.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:17.310
If you let's say enable HTTPS
load balancing that gets

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:18.990
served via an
infrastructure that

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:20.570
has DDoS protection built in.

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:21.320
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:22.986
NIELS PROVOS: Actually,
during the talk,

00:12:22.986 --> 00:12:25.180
I got to share a little
bit that we have extended

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:28.980
that protection, also the news
and human rights organization,

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.439
election monitoring sites,
which seems like a timely topic.

00:12:31.439 --> 00:12:32.938
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That was very cool.

00:12:32.938 --> 00:12:34.770
And I heard the audience
clapping to that.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:35.728
NIELS PROVOS: They did.

00:12:35.728 --> 00:12:37.320
I had not expected
that, to be honest.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:37.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's very nice.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:38.290
MARK MANDEL: No,
that's very cool.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:39.831
And just so we're
clear, because this

00:12:39.831 --> 00:12:42.354
is something I'm interested
in, is it specifically HTTPS?

00:12:42.354 --> 00:12:43.770
Or do you have the
same protection

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:44.811
on like HTTP and network?

00:12:44.811 --> 00:12:48.070
NIELS PROVOS: With the lower
balancer, does HTTP and HTTPS.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:50.150
But to be perfectly
honest, look,

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:52.070
if you're running on
the internet these days,

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:54.060
you'd better protect
yourself with TLS, right?

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:54.810
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.351
NIELS PROVOS: Google
has been pushing

00:12:56.351 --> 00:12:58.340
to encrypt all of our traffic.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:00.090
We have just made the
Transparency Report

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.590
available last week that
sort of tracks our progress.

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:04.340
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:05.798
NIELS PROVOS: And
there's no excuse

00:13:05.798 --> 00:13:08.240
for anybody putting a
website on the internet

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:09.550
not to use encryption.

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:10.030
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:12.488
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Coming from
a responsible for the security

00:13:12.488 --> 00:13:15.782
for Google Cloud Platform,
sounds pretty normal.

00:13:15.782 --> 00:13:16.740
MARK MANDEL: Very good.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.757
Thank you so much for
coming and talking to us.

00:13:18.757 --> 00:13:20.090
NIELS PROVOS: Yeah, my pleasure.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:21.340
Looking forward to it, bye.

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:22.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:24.186
MARK MANDEL: We've been
joined by two speakers

00:13:24.186 --> 00:13:28.030
here at our table, James
Malone and Frances Perry.

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:29.550
Thank you very much
for joining us.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:30.190
FRANCES PERRY: Thank you.

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:31.490
JAMES MALONE: It's
nice to be here.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.340
MARK MANDEL: How are
you both doing today?

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:34.257
JAMES MALONE: Awesome.

00:13:34.257 --> 00:13:35.339
FRANCES PERRY: Doing well.

00:13:35.339 --> 00:13:36.480
Enjoying meeting people.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:37.438
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:13:37.438 --> 00:13:40.590
So I know you were speaking
about some interesting stuff

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:41.810
here at GCP Next.

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:44.060
Do you want to give us a
little brief overview of what

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:45.450
it is you were talking about?

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:46.491
JAMES MALONE: Absolutely.

00:13:46.491 --> 00:13:48.280
So we gave a talk
yesterday that was

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:52.760
focused on creating what
we call next generation

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:56.160
data processing, where
people don't have

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.280
to fight with infrastructure.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:00.335
They don't have to
worry about using

00:14:00.335 --> 00:14:04.040
the multiple tools to try and
do batch and stream processing.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:07.090
And they can trust that
their data pipelines are

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:12.110
going to be portable both on GCP
or between clouds or on cloud

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:13.887
and on-premise.

00:14:13.887 --> 00:14:14.720
FRANCES PERRY: Yeah.

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:16.636
We started with a little
history of MapReduce,

00:14:16.636 --> 00:14:19.240
and how that new programming
paradigm really changed the way

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.310
that we at data processing.

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:23.530
And we talk about how that
diverges a little bit.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:26.150
So inside Google, after that
MapReduce paper was published,

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:27.150
we continued innovating.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:29.050
We had a lot of new
ideas that we kept doing.

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:31.300
But it was this really
homogeneous environment, right?

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:33.095
Within Google, we just
have a few file formats,

00:14:33.095 --> 00:14:33.910
a few languages.

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:35.800
And some very
standardized tooling.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.510
So we really went
deep on the right way

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:38.869
to do data processing.

00:14:38.869 --> 00:14:40.660
But we were able to go
very deep because we

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:42.110
were very homogeneous.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:43.504
Whereas externally,
the ecosystem

00:14:43.504 --> 00:14:44.420
looked very different.

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:47.170
They took the MapReduce paper,
implemented it in Hadoop,

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:49.090
and then this whole
ecosystem flourished

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:51.310
with all these diverse ideas.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:53.030
And now we've got
basically two products

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:55.151
in GCP that build
on that legacy.

00:14:55.151 --> 00:14:56.650
JAMES MALONE: Google
Cloud Dataproc,

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:59.564
which is a managed Spark
and Hadoop offering.

00:14:59.564 --> 00:15:01.480
FRANCES PERRY: And then
Google Cloud Dataflow,

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.515
which is our basically
next generation way

00:15:03.515 --> 00:15:04.580
for writing programs.

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:06.580
It's really going to
combine batch and streaming

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:07.875
into one API.

00:15:07.875 --> 00:15:09.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.220
I got to say that
Google Cloud Dataflow is

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:13.510
one of my favorite products,
to the point that--

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
FRANCES PERRY: Mine too.

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:16.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
will write Java for it.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:17.940
I never write Java.

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.765
And so far, the only
language that is supported

00:15:20.765 --> 00:15:22.480
is Java, so I actually write--

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:22.804
FRANCES PERRY: Ah, ah.

00:15:22.804 --> 00:15:24.530
You need to read your
blogs, my friend.

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:28.395
Yesterday we announced Python
alpha support for batch.

00:15:28.395 --> 00:15:30.659
So the Python SDK is out
there, because we do all

00:15:30.659 --> 00:15:31.950
the development in open source.

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:33.116
So it's out there on GitHub.

00:15:33.116 --> 00:15:35.970
And now we have an alpha
program for service support

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:38.530
to run it on Cloud Dataflow
on the fully managed service.

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:39.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I know
a lot of people that

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:41.172
will be very happy about that.

00:15:41.172 --> 00:15:41.880
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:42.880
You're only one of them.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:44.504
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
will be one of them.

00:15:44.504 --> 00:15:45.430
I prefer Python.

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.970
It's still not Go, but it's
better than Java for me.

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:49.910
That is awesome.

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:51.868
FRANCES PERRY: What we're
really excited about.

00:15:51.868 --> 00:15:54.865
When we donated the data flow
model in these first two SDKs

00:15:54.865 --> 00:15:56.990
that we developed to the
Apache Software Foundation

00:15:56.990 --> 00:15:59.450
as this new incubating
project Apache Beam,

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:02.486
the real goal behind that is
to put the framework out there,

00:16:02.486 --> 00:16:04.735
the programming model, and
then the documentation that

00:16:04.735 --> 00:16:07.650
will allow other developers
who like other languages-- Go

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:09.970
or Scholar or
Ruby-- basically let

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:13.470
people add to this community
and add new languages and also

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:14.000
new runners.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.670
If you've got a different
distributed processing

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:17.380
back end that
you're a fan of, you

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:20.790
can run Beam pipelines on
that, add that capability

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:22.556
into the system.

00:16:22.556 --> 00:16:23.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:24.240
MARK MANDEL: So
you're able to sort

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.425
of leverage that wider
community to help

00:16:26.425 --> 00:16:27.690
build upon that platform.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:28.270
FRANCES PERRY: Right.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:28.520
MARK MANDEL: Exciting.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:29.645
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:16:29.645 --> 00:16:31.744
Can we know a little bit
more about the other side

00:16:31.744 --> 00:16:32.410
of the big data?

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:34.344
Not only Cloud
Dataflow, but data--

00:16:34.344 --> 00:16:35.510
MARK MANDEL: Cloud Dataproc?

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:37.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I was
going to say Dataproc, yeah.

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.341
I always mix Dataproc and
Datalabs for some reason.

00:16:39.341 --> 00:16:39.840
Dataproc.

00:16:39.869 --> 00:16:41.160
FRANCES PERRY: Data everything.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:42.210
JAMES MALONE: Why would
you mix product names that

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:43.871
have data all in them?

00:16:43.871 --> 00:16:45.747
That's just crazy.

00:16:45.747 --> 00:16:47.705
You're obviously not
reading your Google supply

00:16:47.705 --> 00:16:48.870
of flash cards.

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:54.800
So yeah, Cloud Dataproc, it's
built around a different set

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.090
of open source tools.

00:16:56.090 --> 00:16:57.860
So in our talk
yesterday-- and Frances

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.220
just mentioned this--
the MapReduce paper kind

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:02.320
of set off two parallel streams.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.440
And one Google ultimately
led to Cloud Data

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:09.430
And another was the
open source community

00:17:09.430 --> 00:17:12.000
took the MapReduce paper and
created just a whole ecosystem

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:12.500
around it.

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:17.579
So they created Apache Hadoop,
Apache Spark, [INAUDIBLE],

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:18.079
Hive.

00:17:18.079 --> 00:17:21.619
There's probably 30
or 40 different logos.

00:17:21.619 --> 00:17:24.380
And Cloud Dataproc
is designed to allow

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:28.040
people to take advantage of
that open source ecosystem.

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:29.990
But it combines that
open source ecosystem

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:31.650
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:36.300
So I'm looking at mixer, and
there's like only a few knobs

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:37.090
on that.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:39.670
An open source product usually
has a couple hundred knobs

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:41.190
a piece.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:43.650
And Cloud Dataproc is
designed to help people

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:47.490
take advantage of that stuff
without having to be an expert

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:49.940
and buy a ton of
books and know exactly

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:52.152
which memory settings to
do, and all that fun stuff.

00:17:52.152 --> 00:17:54.610
MARK MANDEL: So I can pretty
much go to it and be like, OK,

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:56.600
it's going to give me some
best practices and some boxes

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:57.930
to explain what
certain things are.

00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:59.210
And then I can be
like, boop, boop, boop,

00:17:59.210 --> 00:17:59.870
and then there we go.

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:00.170
JAMES MALONE: Yeah.

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:01.490
So you can go and create a--

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:02.260
FRANCES PERRY: It
makes that noise, too.

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:02.430
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
boop, boop, boop.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:03.221
JAMES MALONE: Yeah.

00:18:03.221 --> 00:18:03.760
Yeah.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:05.390
So, yeah.

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:08.890
You can go and create a
cluster of, like, 100 computers

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.490
all tied together and do
some awesomely parallel data

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:12.505
processing on them.

