00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:48.990
JULIA FERRAIOLI: Well, so,
yesterday at the keynote,

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:52.010
Jeff Dean announced one
of our new platforms,

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.890
which is our machine learning
platform, Cloud Machine

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:55.650
Learning.

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:58.980
And so, my session dove into
a little bit of the details

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:02.390
surrounding what
machine learning can do,

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:06.434
what kind of problems it can
solve, and how does it do that.

00:19:06.434 --> 00:19:08.600
MARK MANDEL: So I believe
one of the problems you're

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:11.034
looking at solving was
something to do with hugs.

00:19:11.034 --> 00:19:13.700
JULIA FERRAIOLI: Well, you know,
sometimes I like a sanity check

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:16.780
here and there, telling me if
I should actually hug something

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:17.500
or not.

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:20.180
Speaking, I'm somebody
who accidentally

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:21.420
hugged a cactus once.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:26.290
So I'm intimately familiar with
things that you shouldn't hug.

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:28.330
So what we were
really trying to do

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:31.860
is do an image
classification problem.

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:36.130
Now, the Vision API, which
is a part of Google's machine

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.750
learning platform, does
things like identify

00:19:38.750 --> 00:19:40.190
what is in an image.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:45.530
But what it can't do is tell
you if you should hug it.

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:46.710
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:47.330
JULIA FERRAIOLI: Naturally.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:47.690
MARK MANDEL: Naturally.

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:49.293
And that's a common
problem I have as well.

00:19:49.293 --> 00:19:50.293
I understand completely.

00:19:50.293 --> 00:19:52.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Huggability
is a very important feature.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:53.898
JULIA FERRAIOLI: Right.

00:19:53.898 --> 00:19:55.620
So what we did was,
I actually sent out

00:19:55.620 --> 00:20:00.100
a survey to my team
asking them to tell me

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:04.080
what are examples of things
that they would or wouldn't hug.

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:07.010
I got some really
interesting answers back.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:10.650
So I went out and I
found example images

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:12.640
of each of those things.

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:15.940
I took about 160
images of things

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:18.020
that people said
that they would hug,

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:21.950
and 160 that they
wouldn't hug, and used

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:26.180
those to train a classifier
that we can use on any image

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:29.930
to give us some information
about whether or not it's

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:33.710
a good idea to hug that object.

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:34.950
MARK MANDEL: So I'm curious.

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:37.140
What results did you get at
the end of it that were either

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:39.010
more interesting or
surprising that you may not

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:39.843
have been expecting?

00:20:39.843 --> 00:20:44.890
JULIA FERRAIOLI: Well, OK,
so I trained the classifier

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:48.520
over things like puppies,
kittens, you know,

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:53.270
the usual suspects, clouds,
dandelions, and pillows.

00:20:53.270 --> 00:20:56.640
So these were things that
people said that they would hug.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:58.070
And it was really
important to get

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:01.040
things that were
organic and inorganic,

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:04.940
right, so that you
get a nice spectrum.

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:08.910
In the not hug category, we
got things like shark's teeth,

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:12.425
broken glass, puffer
fish, stuff like that.

00:21:12.425 --> 00:21:13.880
MARK MANDEL: Makes sense.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.210
JULIA FERRAIOLI:
So I did two tests.

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:21.100
I uploaded a picture of an
octopus from an aquarium.

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:26.160
Now my classifier said,
no, don't hug that, maybe.

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:29.670
Somebody said, E-- too
many hugs, as an error.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:34.320
I didn't actually get that,
but amounts to the same thing.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:38.640
But when I uploaded a
picture of an octopus

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:42.490
that somebody had crocheted,
so like a stuffed animal

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:48.080
octopus, that got a really
nice score saying, yeah,

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:50.230
you should hug that.

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:54.100
And I just thought that was
absolutely fascinating, right?

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:57.180
Because it's taking,
at a high level,

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:01.060
the same subject but with
a different implementation.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:05.100
And it's able to differentiate
between those two.

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:06.837
I thought that was amazing.

00:22:06.837 --> 00:22:08.170
MARK MANDEL: That's pretty cool.

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:09.316
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:22:09.316 --> 00:22:14.240
So neural network, modeling
the huggability of stuff.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:15.480
That is amazing.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:19.030
JULIA FERRAIOLI: I don't believe
it's been done before, I think.

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:20.320
This might be new.

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:22.457
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm
pretty sure it is.

00:22:22.457 --> 00:22:24.850
I never heard about
someone who was like, yeah,

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:26.250
how huggable are things?

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:27.370
That's amazing.

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:27.590
JULIA FERRAIOLI: Yeah.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:28.180
Yeah.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:28.750
Yeah.

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:30.083
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's very cool.

