00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:25.390
be the question of the week.

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:26.190
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:27.439
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So hi, Romin.

00:29:27.439 --> 00:29:28.415
ROMIN IRANI: Hi.

00:29:28.415 --> 00:29:29.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Go for it.

00:29:29.540 --> 00:29:30.630
What is your question?

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.005
ROMIN IRANI: Yeah,
so my question

00:29:32.005 --> 00:29:35.600
is about using Go on App Engine.

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:39.220
You know, in previous, I've
been using App Engine with Java

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:40.270
and other languages.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:42.330
And there's always
been this restriction

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:44.850
that you can't use certain
libraries and App Engine

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:47.670
as a fixed programming model.

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:50.235
So when it comes to Go,
are there any restrictions

00:29:50.235 --> 00:29:52.330
for Go on App Engine?

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:55.580
What would be certain scenarios
in which Go on App Engine

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:58.500
is probably preferred compared
to Go on maybe Compute

00:29:58.500 --> 00:29:59.570
Engine directly?

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:01.653
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, that
is a very good question.

00:30:01.653 --> 00:30:04.530
So to answer the first
part of the question, which

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:07.832
is, what restrictions do we
have on Go for App Engine,

00:30:07.832 --> 00:30:09.540
they're pretty much
the same restrictions

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:11.500
that you can find for
Java on App Engine.

00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:13.690
Meaning that every
request has to be answered

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:15.410
in less than 60 seconds.

00:30:15.410 --> 00:30:19.030
You cannot write to the
file system directly.

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:22.100
And you cannot have
binary libraries.

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.200
Basically, you can only run Go.

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:27.180
And actually on top of that, you
cannot use the unsafe package,

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:29.660
because the unsafe package
is not really safe,

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:30.720
hence the name.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:33.070
MARK MANDEL: There is
also some limitations,

00:30:33.070 --> 00:30:35.170
which is pretty similar,
again, in terms of if you

00:30:35.170 --> 00:30:36.640
want to make HTTP requests.

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:39.286
You have to use the
URL Fetch library.

00:30:39.286 --> 00:30:40.910
And there's some
other stuff like that.

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:43.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But the
URL library, actually,

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:46.225
the URL Fetch library
also provides HTTP client

00:30:46.225 --> 00:30:46.850
if you need to.

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:49.430
So it integrates quite well
with existing libraries

00:30:49.430 --> 00:30:50.400
if you need to.

00:30:50.400 --> 00:30:52.790
So for the second part
of the question, which

00:30:52.790 --> 00:30:55.630
is, when could I
use Go on App Engine

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:57.950
or on Compute Engine,
what I could say

00:30:57.950 --> 00:31:00.940
is if you're doing
web server stuff,

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:02.810
I could always go
with App Engine.

00:31:02.810 --> 00:31:06.420
And that's mainly because
you're getting all the scaling

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:09.050
and zero management for free.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:10.870
You're getting
that automatically,

00:31:10.870 --> 00:31:12.130
which is really cool.

00:31:12.130 --> 00:31:16.910
And if you have something which
is really similar to web server

00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:21.500
but you need something specific
that is a limit-- for instance,

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:24.070
you need to use, I don't
know, regular expressions,

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:26.843
and regular expressions, you
want specific version written

00:31:26.843 --> 00:31:30.240
in C, which is something
that we have, then

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:33.760
you will need to move to
Managed VMs, for instance.

00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:37.950
So you still have that
scalability and the close

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:39.240
to zero management.

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:44.350
But you're now using
C or the file system

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:45.690
or whatever you need.

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:48.120
And otherwise--
yeah, Compute Engine,

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:50.400
I could do it when it's
not really a web server.

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:53.950
Like, if it's a worker doing
something like heavy processing

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:55.912
and it takes long time
and it's communicating

00:31:55.912 --> 00:31:57.370
through a Pub/Sub,
stuff like that.

00:31:57.370 --> 00:31:59.510
MARK MANDEL: Even then, you
could do that with Managed VMs.

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:02.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Even then, you
could do it with Managed VMs

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:03.570
if you really needed to.

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:09.070
So far, what I do is I start
with App Engine by default.

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:11.926
And if I cannot really do it
on App Engine but it's really,

00:32:11.926 --> 00:32:13.300
really close, like
a small thing,

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:15.150
then I consider Managed VMs.

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:17.110
Otherwise, if it
doesn't really match,

00:32:17.110 --> 00:32:19.050
I will start with
Compute Engine.

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:21.583
But really quick, I will
move to Container Engine

00:32:21.583 --> 00:32:23.166
because it's so much
easier to manage.

00:32:23.166 --> 00:32:24.060
MARK MANDEL: I would
agree with that.

00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:26.070
ROMIN IRANI: So can I just
follow up with a slight--

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:27.278
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, go for it.

00:32:27.278 --> 00:32:30.566
ROMIN IRANI: What about
concurrency stuff on Go?

00:32:30.566 --> 00:32:32.440
If you try to run those
things on App Engine,

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:32.800
how does it work?

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:35.008
MARK MANDEL: There is some
limitations on App Engine.

00:32:35.008 --> 00:32:37.050
There is a single thread
for running goroutines

00:32:37.050 --> 00:32:39.270
on App Engine, and
that's just the one.

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:41.120
Generally speaking,
from my experience,

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:44.760
it's never really
been a huge issue.

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:46.250
Especially for web
stuff, it's not

00:32:46.250 --> 00:32:47.600
like you're going to be
doing that much stuff.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:49.530
If you need to expand
upon that, then, yes,

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:52.422
start looking to go further down
that abstraction pathway to go

00:32:52.422 --> 00:32:54.124
to Managed VMs.

00:32:54.124 --> 00:32:55.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:57.900
I could say that the biggest
restriction is that you

00:32:57.900 --> 00:32:59.370
can only run one thread.

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:01.700
But that doesn't mean you
can only run one goroutine.

00:33:01.700 --> 00:33:04.400
You can run as many
goroutines as you need.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:08.920
Important thing is that all
the goroutines will be stopped

00:33:08.920 --> 00:33:12.290
when the HTTP handler finishes.

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:14.970
So you cannot have one goroutine
that is started by the handler

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:16.752
and keeps on running
for one hour.

00:33:16.752 --> 00:33:18.210
That's not something
that we allow.

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:18.580
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:18.580 --> 00:33:20.210
But then you'd just
use task queues.

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:20.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:33:20.440 --> 00:33:21.648
Then you can use task queues.

00:33:21.648 --> 00:33:24.286
And then in task queues, again,
you can use as many goroutines

00:33:24.286 --> 00:33:24.786
as you want.

00:33:24.786 --> 00:33:26.222
So yeah.

